{\"response\": 20, \"explanation\": \"Analysis of 601 respondents shows no evidence that having children decreases extramarital affairs. Unadjusted comparisons indicate higher engagement among those with children: mean affair frequency 1.67 vs 0.91 and proportion with any affair 28.6% vs 15.8% (Mann–Whitney p=0.0012; Welch t-test p=0.0047; chi-square p=0.0015). In a logistic regression predicting any affair with controls for gender, age, years married, religiosity, education, occupation, and marital satisfaction, the children indicator is positive but not statistically significant (coef=0.398, OR=1.49, p=0.173). Thus, the data do not support a decrease; if anything, the unadjusted pattern is in the opposite direction. A low Likert score (20/100) reflects a clear \"No\" with modest uncertainty after adjustment.\"}
